13:06:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: # Color on Screens vs. Color from Reflection You're right that color perception often comes from how objects absorb and reflect different wavelengths of light. A red apple looks red because it absorbs most wavelengths but reflects red light back to your eyes. Screens work differently—they're **emitting** light directly rather than reflecting it. ## The Core Trick: Your Eyes Are the Loophole Screens exploit a quirk of human vision. Your eye has three types of cone cells, each most sensitive to roughly red, green, or blue wavelengths. Your brain determines color by comparing the signals from these three cone types. Screens exploit this by using only **three colors of light** (red, green, blue subpixels) and mixing them in different intensities to trick your cones into perceiving colors that aren't actually present as single wavelengths. For example: - Red + Green light together → your brain perceives "yellow" (even though no yellow wavelength exists) - All three at full intensity → white - All three off → black - Varying intensities → the full spectrum of colors you see This is called **additive color mixing**—you're adding different colored lights together, unlike mixing paint (subtractive), where you're removing wavelengths. ## The Physical Hardware Zoom into any screen and you'll find millions of tiny **subpixels**, grouped in sets of three (R, G, B), each pixel being one such group. **LCD screens:** - A backlight (usually white LED) sits behind everything - Liquid crystals act as tiny shutters, twisting to block or allow light through - Colored filters (red, green, blue) sit in front of each subpixel, letting only that wavelength through - By controlling how much light passes through each filter, you control the color **OLED screens:** - Each subpixel is its own light source (organic compounds that emit light when electricity passes through) - No backlight needed—pixels can turn completely off (true black) - Red, green, blue OLED subpixels are individually controlled ## Putting It Together When you see "purple" on screen, there's no purple light actually being emitted. Instead: - Red subpixel: bright - Blue subpixel: bright - Green subpixel: off These three tiny light sources are so close together that your eye can't resolve them individually at normal viewing distances—they blend into a single perceived color. ## The Key Difference - **Reflected color** (apples, paint): Light already exists; the surface **subtracts** wavelengths by absorbing them. - **Screen color**: No white light source needs to hit anything. The screen **directly emits** specific wavelengths and adds them together, exploiting your visual system's three-receptor design. This is why screens can only show colors within a certain "gamut" (range)—they're limited by how accurately three colors of light can trick your particular type of cone cells into perceiving the whole rainbow.
