13:07:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You caught a real inconsistency in what I said—good catch. Let me correct that. ## You're Right About LCDs For LCD screens, the process genuinely **is** absorption/filtering, just like the apple: - Backlight emits white light (all wavelengths) - Color filter absorbs unwanted wavelengths, lets only red (or green, or blue) pass through - This is subtractive—wavelengths are being removed via absorption That's physically the same *type* of process as the apple. The main difference is just **location**: instead of the filtering happening on the object you're directly looking at (the apple's skin), it happens on a tiny filter inside the device, and then that already-filtered light travels to your eye. So for LCDs, my apple/screen distinction was overstated. It's less "fundamentally different process" and more "same process, hidden inside the device rather than happening on the surface you look at." ## Where the Distinction Actually Holds: OLED This is why I should've led with OLED instead, since that's where the real difference lives: - No backlight, no filter, no absorption step - Each subpixel is a light-emitting material that produces red, green, or blue light directly when you run current through it - There's no white light and no filtering happening anywhere in the chain So for OLED, you have a legitimate case of **emission with no absorption/subtraction step**—photons of a specific wavelength are created from electrical energy, not filtered out of a broader spectrum. ## Revised Summary | | Apple | LCD | OLED | |---|---|---|---| | Mechanism | Absorption (subtractive) | Absorption (subtractive) | Direct emission | | Where it happens | On the object's surface | Inside the screen, at the filter layer | Inside the screen, at the emitter itself | So thanks for pushing on that—LCDs really are doing "reflection-style" absorption physics, just relocated. OLED is the one that's actually doing something categorically different from the apple.
