13:08:52 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: Yeah, their core point holds up well—most light-emitting sources we encounter do work through that filtering mechanism, with OLED being the notable exception. Though there are other natural examples worth mentioning: bioluminescence, fire, stars, lava, and lightning all emit light directly rather than filtering it. And I should clarify that LCDs, which still dominate most screens, actually do operate on that same reflection-and-filtering principle we discussed, so their observation really does capture how the majority of displays work.
13:08:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes, your original point holds up as the general rule. Let's confirm it clearly: ## Your Point Was Right All Along For the vast majority of color you encounter in daily life—skin, clothes, walls, plants, food, most objects—color absolutely does come from **selective absorption and reflection of existing light**. No light source, no color; turn off all lights in a room and everything looks black, because there's no light to selectively reflect. This is true for: - Biological pigments (melanin in skin, chlorophyll in plants, etc.) - Dyes and pigments in fabric/paint - Most manufactured objects ## Where the Exceptions Live You're also right that emission is the rarer case, but it's worth knowing where it shows up, since it's not *just* OLEDs: - **Fire, lava, stars, lightbulb filaments** — incandescence (heat-driven light emission) - **Bioluminescence** — fireflies, certain deep-sea fish, some fungi (chemical reactions producing light) - **Neon signs, plasma** — electrically excited gases emitting light - **OLED screens** — electroluminescence, as we discussed - **The sun/stars generally** — nuclear fusion producing light directly So emission is a real, if smaller, category of "how color happens" in nature—not *exclusively* a manufactured oddity, but definitely the minority case compared to reflection. ## One Correction Worth Remembering Also worth flagging again: even in screens, **LCDs** (still probably the majority of screens in the world—most budget TVs, monitors, phones) use the filtering/absorption method, not emission. So even within "how screens work," reflection-style absorption is doing a lot of the work—it's not purely an OLED vs. everything-else split. ## Bottom Line Your instinct that "color = reflected light off materials" is a great general model for describing color in the world. Emissive color (OLED, fire, bioluminescence, etc.) is a real but smaller category where light is generated rather than filtered—worth knowing about, but not overturning your original mental model as the dominant explanation for most color you see.
